<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chanakya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 375–c. 283 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Kautilya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chanakya, also known as Kautilya, was an ancient Indian scholar and statesman traditionally regarded as the chief adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>. He is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a>, a treatise on statecraft, economics and military strategy. Tradition holds that he guided Chandragupta's overthrow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda dynasty</a> and the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chanakya.jpg" alt="Chanakya" width="299" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chanakya_artistic_depiction.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>About 321 BCE Chandragupta Maurya, traditionally guided by the Brahmin <b>Chanakya</b>, overthrew the last Nanda king and founded the Maurya Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Guided, according to tradition, by the Brahmin strategist <b>Chanakya</b> (also known as Kautilya), Chandragupta built a realm that stretched from the Ganges plain to the north-western frontier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kautilya's</b> Arthashastra describes the Kambojas as a sangha whose members lived by trade and arms, suggesting rule by a warrior aristocracy rather than a king.</blockquote><a href="/state/kamboja">Kamboja</a></li><li><blockquote>Taxila was a wealthy city on the trade route from Bactria to the Ganges and a famous centre of learning; tradition says <b>Chanakya</b>, adviser to Chandragupta Maurya, taught there.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-taxila">Kingdom of Taxila</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 322 BCE Chandragupta Maurya, guided according to tradition by the Brahmin <b>Chanakya</b>, overthrew Dhana Nanda and founded the Maurya Empire, which inherited the Nanda administration and capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/nanda-empire">Nanda Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.75,72.79&amp;year=-316" aria-label="Taxila on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6407 940 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Chanakya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6857 1210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6857 1210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.75,72.79&amp;year=-316">What was here in 316 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In the happiness of his subjects lies his happiness; in their welfare his welfare; whatever pleases himself he shall not consider as good, but whatever pleases his subjects he shall consider as good.&#8221;</p><footer>Arthashastra, book 1, chapter 19 &#183; tr. R. Shamasastry, 1915 (2nd ed. 1923) &#183; the Arthashastra is traditionally ascribed to him (as Kautilya) &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/india/kautilya1.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If a king is energetic, his subjects will be equally energetic. If he is reckless, they will not only be reckless likewise, but also eat into his works.&#8221;</p><footer>Arthashastra, book 1, chapter 19 &#183; tr. R. Shamasastry, 1915 (2nd ed. 1923) &#183; the Arthashastra is traditionally ascribed to him (as Kautilya) &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/india/kautilya1.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;As the duty of a king consists in protecting his subjects with justice, its observance leads him to heaven. He who does not protect his people or upsets the social order wields his royal scepter in vain.&#8221;</p><footer>Arthashastra, book 3, chapter 1 &#183; tr. R. Shamasastry, 1915 (2nd ed. 1923) &#183; the Arthashastra is traditionally ascribed to him (as Kautilya) &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/india/kautilya1.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Sovereignty is possible only with assistance. A single wheel can never move. Hence he shall employ ministers and hear their opinion.&#8221;</p><footer>Arthashastra, book 1, chapter 7 &#183; tr. R. Shamasastry, 1915 (2nd ed. 1923) &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/india/kautilya1.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…when a king makes himself inaccessible to his people and entrusts his work to his immediate officers, he may be sure to engender confusion in business, and to cause thereby public disaffection, and himself a prey to his enemies.&#8221;</p><footer>Arthashastra, book 1, chapter 19 &#183; tr. R. Shamasastry, 1915 (2nd ed. 1923) &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/india/kautilya1.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;All urgent calls he shall hear at once, but never put off, for when postponed, they will prove too hard or impossible to accomplish.&#8221;</p><footer>Arthashastra, book 1, chapter 19 &#183; tr. R. Shamasastry, 1915 (2nd ed. 1923) &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/india/kautilya1.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
